{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "**the social network (2010) – directed by david fincher**\n\nthe social network is a razor-sharp, meticulously crafted film that dissects ambition, betrayal, and the genesis of facebook with the precision of a scalpel. david fincher’s direction is as coldly elegant as ever, blending sleek cinematography by jeff cronenweth with a soundtrack by trent reznor and atticus ross that feels like the hum of a computer server room—methodical, almost hypnotic. the film is a masterclass in tension, where every line of dialogue, every cut, and every shadow on a face carries weight.\n\njonah hill’s screenplay is dense with sharp, witty dialogue, but it’s the performances that truly anchor the picture. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining turn as mark zuckerberg, capturing the genius and narcissism of the young billionaire with unsettling accuracy. his zuckerberg is not just a likable underdog but a deeply flawed protagonist—brilliant yet cruel, charismatic yet socially stunted. the supporting cast is equally strong: armie hammer as edward snowden (though the name is misused here for edward leo, zuckerberg’s roommate) radiates quiet menace, while andrew garfield as divya narendra and max minghella as chris hughes provide compelling counterpoints to zuckerberg’s worldview. jessica chastain’s benedict cumberbatch-ish erin walsh is a standout—her performance is magnetic, oscillating between vulnerability and fury, and her scenes with eisenberg are electric with chemistry.\n\nthe film’s structure is non-linear, jumping between key moments in zuckerberg’s early life—harvard years, harvard lawsuit, early facebook days—and while the jumps can feel disjointed at first, fincher and hill use them to emphasize the cyclical nature of zuckerberg’s obsessive behavior. the pacing is relentless, refusing to linger on sentimentality. even the romantic subplot feels secondary to the icy ambition at the film’s core.\n\nvisually, the movie is a study in contrast—warm, sunlit campus scenes juxtaposed with the sterile glow of zuckerberg’s dorm room or the cold, corporate lighting of the early facebook offices. the famous \"you have the right to remain silent\" scene, where zuckerberg sits in a deposition room reciting his own manifesto, is a chilling moment that encapsulates the film’s themes: power, manipulation, and the cost of creation.\n\nthe social network isn’t just about the birth of a tech empire; it’s about the loneliness of genius and the way ideas—like people—can be both worshipped and exploited. fincher and hill avoid the trap of making zuckerberg a villain or a hero, instead presenting him as a man shaped by his circumstances, his flaws, and his unshakable belief in his own superiority. it’s a film that feels eerily prescient, not just about the rise of social media but about the increasingly detached, transactional nature of modern human interaction.\n\nin the end, the social network is a triumph—a film that’s as intellectually stimulating as it is visually stunning, and as emotionally complex as it is narratively precise. it’s a movie that demands to be watched with full attention, rewarding the viewer with layers of meaning in every frame. fincher’s return to features after the near-fatal production of the cursed the curious case of benjamin button (2008) is nothing short of a rebirth, and this film cements him as one of the most essential directors of his generation. ten out of ten—flawless.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -1.9109424352645874}, {"token": "the", "logprob": -0.10903476178646088}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.07709474116563797}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -0.00025459862081333995}, {"token": " (", "logprob": -0.10300657153129578}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.009290085174143314}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -3.218599158572033e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.6569954823353328e-05}, 